# Merlin has no .dockerignore and does not need one. Peregrine does, and the
# reason was the transcription assets: the working tree was around 692 MB, almost
# all of it the whisper model and the bundled ffmpeg binary, and `COPY . .` would
# push every byte of that into the builder layer on every build. CI builds the
# image twice (docker-build and push-image) for two architectures, so without this
# file that was roughly 2.7 GB of pointless context per run.
#
# M12 removed the tree, so the working copy is small again. The file stays because
# the reasoning holds for whatever an engine implementation brings back, and
# because excluding the corpus and .env from the build context is worth doing on
# its own.
#
# The build only needs Go sources, go.mod and go.sum. Everything else here is
# excluded on purpose.

.git
.github
.claude
.gitignore

# Secrets and runtime data must never enter an image layer, even transiently:
# a layer is a permanent, distributable copy.
.env
blocklist.txt
markov.db
markov.db.lock
*.db.tmp
backups/

# The transcription assets, if an engine implementation brings them back.
voicenotes/

# Build output from a host build, which would otherwise shadow the one the
# builder stage produces.
/bin/
/dist/
peregrine
peregrine.exe

*.log

# Docs are excluded from the build context but deliberately NOT from the prose
# CI job, which runs against the checkout rather than the image.
*.md
LICENSE
docker-compose*.yml
Dockerfile
